+\title{\textbf{Contender 20: W-Rank Hyperreflection in BusyLean}\\[0.45em]
+  \large A second-order, well-founded-tree closure of diagonal transformer lifting}
+\author{Research proposal prepared for Vladimir Reshetnikov}
+\date{October 13, 2026}
+
+\begin{document}
+\maketitle
+
+\begin{abstract}
+BusyLean's current Contender~19 combines two independent sources of growth: a
+large-Veblen-length ladder of normalizing universes and a finite hierarchy of
+\emph{transformer lifts}.  Its lift rank is a natural number, albeit the
+astronomical value \(\mathrm{family18}(n)+1\).  This note proposes Contender~20,
+which raises the amplifier one type level.  A \emph{hyperlift} maps transformer
+lifts to transformer lifts.  We define a structural recursion that iterates a
+hyperlift along an arbitrary countably branching well-founded tree.  At each
+outer node, the recursively generated child lifts are spliced into a single
+lift, after which one diagonal rank step is applied.  This is a W-tree analogue
+of transfinite iteration with a genuine limit constructor, rather than another
+finite self-composition.
+
+The central technical point is strict growth without monotonicity.  Reflection
+is not monotone in its oracle: a reflected program may inspect an oracle value
+and branch.  Consequently, none of the comparison proofs may replace an
+oracle by a pointwise larger one.  We instead maintain two explicit invariants:
+a lift preserves progressive transformers, and it strictly exceeds its input
+transformer at every visible coordinate.  We prove that splicing preserves
+both invariants, one rank step preserves both, W-tree iteration preserves both,
+and every nonempty stage---including a leaf stage---strictly exceeds its seed
+lift.  The node proof follows child zero and then coordinate zero of the splice;
+it uses no extensional monotonicity principle.
+
+At input \(n\), the finite seed lift already has rank
+\(\mathrm{family19}(n)+1\).  The outer rank is an autonomous hyperclosure above
+a schedule that is itself an autonomous hyperclosure above Contender~19's
+schedule.  Applying the resulting W-rank lift to the large-Veblen reflector and
+the constant oracle family \(\mathrm{family19}\) yields \(F_{20}(n)\), and
+\(\mathrm{contender}_{20}=F_{20}(F_{20}(42))\).  The supplied Lean development
+proves pointwise \(\mathrm{family19}(n)<F_{20}(n)\), hence
+\(\mathrm{contender}_{19}<\mathrm{contender}_{20}\), and also bounds every
+large-Veblen-ladder program in the existing depth-\(n\) enumeration.
+
+The package is designed for BusyLean's Lean~4.34.0 toolchain and includes the
+three Lean modules, focused audit and smoke files, an integration patch, and
+static checks.  The present execution environment did not contain Lean, so the
+sources have not been kernel-checked here; this verification boundary is stated
+explicitly throughout the report.
+\end{abstract}
