+%======================================================================
+\section{Baseline: what Contender 19 already does}
+
+BusyLean is a Lean~4 continuation of the constructive biggest-number contest
+originating in Roux's Coq repository and Aaronson's larger-number essay
+\cite{aaronson,roux}.  A contender is not merely a description.  It is a closed
+\code{Nat}, computable in principle, together with a formal theorem that it is
+larger than the incumbent.  The repository additionally audits axioms,
+dependency closure, and clean-build time.
+
+Contender~19 has the form
+\[
+F_{19}(n)=D_{S_{19}(n),\,r_{19}(n)}(L)
+  (\lambda i.\,\mathrm{family18})(n,n),
+\qquad
+\mathrm{contender}_{19}=F_{19}(F_{19}(42)),
+\]
+where:
+\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
+\item \(L=\code{lTransformer}\) reflects over the level-\(n\) language whose
+  atoms are normal large-Veblen terms;
+\item \(S_{19}(n)\) is an autonomous W-tree schedule above the earlier index;
+\item \(D_{S,k}=\code{diagonalLift S k}\) is a natural-number hierarchy of
+  transformer lifts;
+\item \(r_{19}(n)=\mathrm{family18}(n)+1\); and
+\item \(\mathrm{family18}(n)=F_{18}(F_{18}(n))\).
+\end{itemize}
+
+The language side is already formidable.  Its atoms are Sch\"utte-style
+Klammersymbole: Veblen argument arrays whose positions are themselves terms.
+The repository proves well-foundedness of the normal forms and then obtains a
+normalizing universe ladder from the generic theorem \code{FL.wfUniv}.  In
+particular, a new contender cannot honestly advertise a major advance merely
+by replacing 42 with 43 or adding one more self-composition.
+
+The amplifier side, however, has a clean boundary.  \code{diagonalLift} is
+indexed by \(k\in\N\):
+\begin{align*}
+D_{S,0}(T)&=\code{liftTransformer}(T),\\
+D_{S,k+1}(T)&=\code{stageLift}(D_{S,k})(D_{S,k}(T))(S).
+\end{align*}
+Even when \(k\) is incomprehensibly large, the rank object is still a natural
+number.  Contender~20 targets precisely this boundary.
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{Design criteria and rejected easy moves}
+
+A satisfactory successor should meet five criteria.
+
+\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
+\item \textbf{Closed and constructive.}  Every definition must reduce by
+  structural recursion or already certified normalization; no unbounded search
+  for a witness, semantic truth predicate, or Busy Beaver is permitted.
+\item \textbf{A structural advance.}  The definition should add a new closure
+  principle, not only a larger numeral in an existing parameter.
+\item \textbf{A direct formal comparison.}  The previous family should occur as
+  a computable seed, while the proof should expose strict occurrences rather
+  than rely on unproved growth folklore.
+\item \textbf{Compatibility with nonmonotone reflection.}  Reflected programs
+  can branch on oracle values, so pointwise oracle monotonicity is unavailable.
+\item \textbf{Small trusted surface.}  The new proof should be generic and sit
+  above the existing normalization theorems rather than duplicate the
+  large-Veblen development.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+Several tempting moves were rejected.
+
+\paragraph{Only seed Contender 19 into Contender 19's amplifier.}
+Replacing \code{family18} by \code{family19} and retaining exactly the old
+finite-rank construction would certainly give a larger number and a short
+proof.  It would also be a routine generational step.  The present proposal
+uses that stronger seed \emph{and} adds an independent W-rank closure.
+
+\paragraph{Add more finite self-compositions.}
+Definitions such as \(F_{19}^{F_{19}(42)}(42)\) are computable and enormous,
+but they do not exploit BusyLean's generic reflection architecture.  They also
+obscure the conceptual comparison between language strength and amplifier
+strength.
+
+\paragraph{Jump immediately to collapsing-function atoms.}
+A Bachmann--Howard or stronger ladder is a natural language-side project, but
+raw collapsing-function syntax is not automatically well-founded under the
+intended comparison.  Canonical closure side conditions are essential, and a
+credible formalization is substantially larger than the present overlay.  It
+is a valuable future direction, not a prudent source of a quick theorem.
+
+\paragraph{Assume monotonicity.}
+This is simply invalid.  If a reflected term reads an oracle value and branches,
+a larger oracle can select a smaller branch.  Existing BusyLean proofs avoid
+this trap, and the new second-order proofs preserve that discipline.
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{Four type levels of reflection data}
+
+The construction is easiest to understand by separating four function spaces.
+
+\begin{definition}[Families]
+\[
+\Fam := \N\to\N\to\N.
+\]
+A family \(s\) is read as a countable collection of unary functions:
+\(s(i,n)\) is coordinate \(i\) at numerical input \(n\).
+\end{definition}
+
+\begin{definition}[Transformers]
+\[
+\Tr := \Fam\to\Fam.
+\]
+A transformer maps an oracle family to a new family.  The repository's
+large-Veblen reflection transformer is one example.
+\end{definition}
+
+\begin{definition}[Transformer lifts]
+\[
+\Lift := \Tr\to\Tr.
+\]
+A transformer lift amplifies a transformer.  Existing examples are
+\code{liftTransformer}, \code{stageLift L}, and every
+\code{diagonalLift schedule k}.
+\end{definition}
+
+\begin{definition}[Hyperlifts]
+\[
+\HLift := \Lift\to\Lift.
+\]
+This is the new level.  A hyperlift amplifies the method by which transformers
+are amplified.
+\end{definition}
+
+The Lean declarations mirror this hierarchy:
+
+\begin{lstlisting}
+abbrev Transformer := Family → Family
+abbrev TransformerLift := Transformer → Transformer
+abbrev HyperLift := TransformerLift → TransformerLift
+\end{lstlisting}
+
+A type-level rise of this kind has precedent inside BusyLean.  Contender~6
+iterated ordinary reflection over families.  Contender~12 lifted reflection to
+transformers.  Contender~15 introduced a finite hierarchy of transformer
+lifts.  Contender~20 moves from a natural-number hierarchy in \(\Lift\) to
+well-founded-tree recursion in \(\HLift\).
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{The invariants: progress and strict occurrence}
+
+The first-order invariant already present in BusyLean is progressivity.
+
+\begin{definition}[Progressive transformer]
+A transformer \(T\) is progressive when
+\[
+\Prog(T) :\Longleftrightarrow
+\forall s,i,o,n\;\bigl(i\le n\Rightarrow s(i,n)<T(s)(o,n)\bigr).
+\]
+Every visible seed coordinate is strictly below every output coordinate at the
+same numerical input.
+\end{definition}
+
+For a lift \(L\in\Lift\), two properties matter.
+
+\begin{definition}[Preservation and strict inflation]
+\begin{align*}
+\mathsf{Pres}(L)&:\Longleftrightarrow
+  \forall T\;(\Prog(T)\Rightarrow\Prog(L(T))),\\
+\mathsf{Strict}(L)&:\Longleftrightarrow
+  \forall T\;\Bigl(\Prog(T)\Rightarrow
+  \forall s,i,o,n\;(i\le n\Rightarrow T(s)(i,n)<L(T)(s)(o,n))\Bigr).
+\end{align*}
+Set \(\Good(L):=\mathsf{Pres}(L)\wedge\mathsf{Strict}(L)\).
+\end{definition}
+
+In Lean these are the existing predicates \code{PreservesProgressive} and
+\code{StrictlyInflationary}, paired by the new abbreviation:
+
+\begin{lstlisting}
+abbrev GoodLift (lift : TransformerLift) : Prop :=
+  PreservesProgressive lift ∧ StrictlyInflationary lift
+\end{lstlisting}
+
+At the hyperlift level we require preservation of goodness and a second-order
+strictness statement.
+
+\begin{definition}[Goodness-preserving and second-order strict]
+For \(H\in\HLift\),
+\begin{align*}
+\mathsf{PresGood}(H)&:\Longleftrightarrow
+  \forall L\;(\Good(L)\Rightarrow\Good(H(L))),\\
+\mathsf{Strict}_2(H)&:\Longleftrightarrow
+  \forall L\;\bigl[\Good(L)\Rightarrow{}\\
+ &\qquad \forall T\;\bigl[\Prog(T)\Rightarrow{}\\
+ &\qquad\quad \forall s,i,o,n\;\bigl(i\le n\Rightarrow
+    L(T)(s)(i,n)<H(L)(T)(s)(o,n)\bigr)\bigr]\bigr].
+\end{align*}
+\end{definition}
+
+These predicates are intentionally intensional: they identify a concrete copy
+of the old object inside the new one.  That is why they remain useful when no
+monotonicity theorem exists.
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{Splicing lifts: the limit mechanism}
+
+The repository already has \code{spliceTransformers}.  Given transformers
+\(T_0,T_1,\ldots\), the splice selects \(T_i\) by output coordinate \(i\), then
+reads its canonical coordinate zero.  We lift this operation one type level.
+
+\begin{definition}[Lift splice]
+For \(L_i\in\Lift\), define
+\[
+\mathsf{spliceLift}(L_0,L_1,\ldots)(T)
+:=\mathsf{spliceTransformer}(L_0(T),L_1(T),\ldots).
+\]
+Equivalently,
+\[
+\mathsf{spliceLift}(\vec L)(T)(s)(i,n)=L_i(T)(s)(0,n).
+\]
+\end{definition}
+
+\begin{lstlisting}
+def spliceLifts (lifts : Nat → TransformerLift) : TransformerLift :=
+  fun base => spliceTransformers (fun i => lifts i base)
+\end{lstlisting}
+
+\begin{proposition}[Splicing preserves goodness]\label{prop:splice}
+If every \(L_i\) is good, then \(\mathsf{spliceLift}(\vec L)\) is good.
+\end{proposition}
+
+\begin{proof}
+Let \(T\) be progressive.  Each \(L_i(T)\) is progressive, so the existing
+first-order splice theorem makes their splice progressive.
+
+For strictness, fix seed \(s\), input coordinate \(j\), output coordinate
+\(i\), and \(j\le n\).  Strictness of \(L_i\), with canonical output coordinate
+zero, gives
+\[
+T(s)(j,n)<L_i(T)(s)(0,n)
+=\mathsf{spliceLift}(\vec L)(T)(s)(i,n).
+\]
+No comparison between two different oracle families occurs.
+\end{proof}
+
+The splice is the correct analogue of a limit-stage join.  It does not collapse
+all children to a numerical maximum.  Every child remains addressable, which
+allows later transformers to select branches dynamically through the family
+interface.
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{One diagonal step at the hyperlift level}
+
+Fix an inner schedule \(S\in\WTree(\N)\).  The new hyperlift
+\(\Step_S\) sends a lift \(L\) to
+\[
+\Step_S(L)(T):=\code{stageLift}\bigl(L,L(T),S\bigr).
+\]
+
+\begin{lstlisting}
+def rankStep (schedule : WTree Nat) : HyperLift :=
+  fun lift base => stageLift lift (lift base) schedule
+\end{lstlisting}
+
+This is exactly the successor functional implicit in \code{diagonalLift}: the
+input lift appears twice, once to pre-amplify the base transformer and once as
+the local lifting operation used at every node of the schedule.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Rank step preserves goodness]\label{prop:rankgood}
+If \(L\) is good, then \(\Step_S(L)\) is good.
+\end{proposition}
+
+\begin{proof}
+Let \(T\) be progressive.  Preservation for \(L\) gives progressivity of
+\(L(T)\).  The existing theorem \code{stageLift\_progressive} now shows that
+\(\code{stageLift}(L,L(T),S)\) is progressive.
+
+For strictness, use two explicit strict inclusions:
+\[
+T(s)(i,n)<L(T)(s)(i,n)
+<\code{stageLift}(L,L(T),S)(s)(o,n).
+\]
+The first is strictness of \(L\); the second is
+\code{base\_lt\_stageLift}, applied with base transformer \(L(T)\).
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{proposition}[Second-order strictness]\label{prop:rankstrict2}
+If \(L\) is good and \(T\) is progressive, then
+\[
+L(T)(s)(i,n)<\Step_S(L)(T)(s)(o,n)
+\qquad(i\le n).
+\]
+Thus \(\mathsf{Strict}_2(\Step_S)\) holds.
+\end{proposition}
+
+\begin{proof}
+This is a direct application of \code{base\_lt\_stageLift} with base
+transformer \(L(T)\).  Unlike Proposition~\ref{prop:rankgood}, no preliminary
+step from \(T\) to \(L(T)\) is needed.
+\end{proof}
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{W-tree iteration of hyperlifts}
+
+Let \(H\in\HLift\), let \(L_0\in\Lift\), and let \(A\in\WTree(\N)\).  Define
+\(\Run(H,L_0,A)\in\Lift\) by structural recursion:
+\begin{align*}
+\Run(H,L_0,\mathsf{leaf})
+  &=H(L_0),\\
+\Run(H,L_0,\mathsf{node}(f))
+  &=H\left(\mathsf{spliceLift}
+      \bigl(i\mapsto\Run(H,L_0,f(i))\bigr)\right).
+\end{align*}
+
+\begin{lstlisting}
+def runHyperLift (op : HyperLift) (seed : TransformerLift) :
+    WTree Nat → TransformerLift
+  | .leaf => op seed
+  | .node f =>
+      op (spliceLifts (fun i => runHyperLift op seed (f i)))
+\end{lstlisting}
+
+Because \code{WTree} is inductive, every branch is well-founded even though a
+node has countably many children and their heights may be unbounded.  Hence the
+outer rank can represent genuine limit shapes such as a root whose \(i\)-th
+child has finite height \(i\).  The definition remains ordinary structural
+recursion in Lean.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Goodness of W-tree iteration]\label{thm:rungood}
+If \(H\) preserves good lifts and \(L_0\) is good, then
+\(\Run(H,L_0,A)\) is good for every well-founded tree \(A\).
+\end{theorem}
+
+\begin{proof}
+By structural induction on \(A\).  At a leaf, apply preservation of goodness to
+\(L_0\).  At a node, the induction hypothesis makes every child lift good;
+Proposition~\ref{prop:splice} makes their splice good; preservation of goodness
+for \(H\) finishes the step.
+\end{proof}
+
+The next theorem is the key comparison result.
+
+\begin{theorem}[The W-tree stage strictly exceeds its seed]\label{thm:seedlt}
+Assume \(H\) preserves goodness and is second-order strictly inflationary.  If
+\(L_0\) is good and \(T\) is progressive, then for every \(A\), seed family
+\(s\), and \(i\le n\),
+\[
+L_0(T)(s)(i,n)<\Run(H,L_0,A)(T)(s)(o,n).
+\]
+\end{theorem}
+
+\begin{proof}
+Again use structural induction on \(A\).
+
+At a leaf, the result is exactly \(\mathsf{Strict}_2(H)\).
+
+At a node \(\mathsf{node}(f)\), first follow child zero.  The induction
+hypothesis gives
+\[
+L_0(T)(s)(i,n)<\Run(H,L_0,f(0))(T)(s)(0,n).
+\]
+By definition of lift splicing, the right-hand side is coordinate zero of the
+splice of all child lifts.  Theorem~\ref{thm:rungood} and
+Proposition~\ref{prop:splice} show that this splice is good.  Apply
+\(\mathsf{Strict}_2(H)\) to the splice, from its coordinate zero to arbitrary
+output coordinate \(o\):
+\[
+\Run(H,L_0,f(0))(T)(s)(0,n)
+< H(\mathsf{spliceLift}(\ldots))(T)(s)(o,n).
+\]
+The last term is definitionally
+\(\Run(H,L_0,\mathsf{node}(f))(T)(s)(o,n)\).  Transitivity completes the
+proof.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{remark}[Why child zero is enough]
+The theorem does not attempt to compare every child simultaneously or take a
+maximum over child values.  It needs one explicit embedded predecessor.  Child
+zero supplies that predecessor, while the splice preserves access to all other
+children for subsequent computations.
+\end{remark}
+
+\begin{remark}[No hidden monotonicity]
+The proof never changes the oracle supplied to the same reflected program.
+Every inequality follows a syntactically present old lift or child.  This is the
+second-order analogue of the proof style already used by BusyLean's
+\code{transformer\_lt\_lift\_at} and \code{base\_lt\_stageLift}.
+\end{remark}
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{The generic W-rank diagonal lift}
+
+We specialize the preceding recursion to the diagonal rank step:
+\[
+\mathsf{treeDiagonalLift}(S,A,L_0)
+:=\Run(\Step_S,L_0,A).
+\]
+
+\begin{lstlisting}
+def treeDiagonalLift (schedule rankTree : WTree Nat)
+    (seed : TransformerLift) : TransformerLift :=
+  runHyperLift (rankStep schedule) seed rankTree
+\end{lstlisting}
+
+Propositions~\ref{prop:rankgood} and~\ref{prop:rankstrict2}, together with
+Theorems~\ref{thm:rungood} and~\ref{thm:seedlt}, immediately yield:
+
+\begin{corollary}[Generic W-rank facts]\label{cor:generic}
+If \(L_0\) is good, then
+\(\mathsf{treeDiagonalLift}(S,A,L_0)\) is good and strictly exceeds \(L_0\)
+at every visible coordinate over every progressive base transformer.
+\end{corollary}
+
+This factorization is important for auditability.  The contender-specific file
+does not prove a bespoke inequality through an enormous definition.  It only
+instantiates a small, reusable theorem about arbitrary schedules, rank trees,
+seed lifts, base transformers, and oracle families.
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{Choosing the two Contender-20 trees}
+
+Let
+\[
+\mathrm{family19}(n):=F_{19}(F_{19}(n)).
+\]
+As in earlier BusyLean generations, this is a function of the input, not a
+reference to the closed constant \code{contender\_19}.  Consequently the
+closure audit can require that \code{contender\_20} contain none of the earlier
+closed contender constants.
+
+The repository's \code{Autonomous} syntax builds transparent well-founded trees
+with addition, multiplication, omega powers, derivative-like fixed-point
+closures, a common-critical closure, and higher derivatives.  We use it twice.
+
+\begin{construction}[Inner schedule]
+\begin{align*}
+S_{20}(n):={}&\code{Autonomous.realize}\bigl(
+  \code{Autonomous.hyper}(\mathrm{family19}(n)+42,\\
+&\hspace{41mm}\code{Autonomous.encode}(S_{19}(n)))\bigr).
+\end{align*}
+\end{construction}
+
+\begin{construction}[Outer rank tree]
+\begin{align*}
+R_{20}(n):={}&\code{Autonomous.realize}\bigl(
+  \code{Autonomous.hyper}(\mathrm{family19}(n)+42,\\
+&\hspace{41mm}\code{Autonomous.encode}(S_{20}(n)))\bigr).
+\end{align*}
+\end{construction}
+
+The first closure enlarges the schedule used inside each rank step.  The second
+closure is not another inner schedule: it indexes the recursion over lifts
+itself.  This separation prevents the outer mechanism from collapsing into
+``one more finite rank'' by notation alone.
+
+The concrete Lean code is:
+
+\begin{lstlisting}
+def schedule20 (n : Nat) : WTree Nat :=
+  Autonomous.realize
+    (Autonomous.hyper (family19 n + 42)
+      (Autonomous.encode (schedule19 n)))
+
+def rankTree20 (n : Nat) : WTree Nat :=
+  Autonomous.realize
+    (Autonomous.hyper (family19 n + 42)
+      (Autonomous.encode (schedule20 n)))
+\end{lstlisting}
+
+\begin{caution}[Syntax trees, not asserted ordinal equalities]
+BusyLean's \code{Autonomous} module explicitly treats these operations as
+transparent tree syntax.  The present proposal likewise does not identify
+\code{hyper k a} with a particular quotient ordinal.  The only formal facts
+used are structural well-foundedness of the generated W-trees and the generic
+strict-growth theorems.
+\end{caution}
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{The finite seed lift and the final family}
+
+The outer W-rank iteration starts from a seed that already exceeds the entire
+Contender-19 family pointwise through a large finite rank:
+\[
+D_{20}(n):=\code{diagonalLift}(S_{20}(n),\mathrm{family19}(n)+1).
+\]
+This is \code{seedLift20 n}.  Existing BusyLean theorems give both components
+of \(\Good(D_{20}(n))\).
+
+Define
+\[
+W_{20}(n):=\mathsf{treeDiagonalLift}
+  (S_{20}(n),R_{20}(n),D_{20}(n)).
+\]
+Let \(s_{20}(i,n)=\mathrm{family19}(n)\) be the constant seed family.  Finally,
+\[
+F_{20}(n):=W_{20}(n)(L)(s_{20})(n,n),
+\qquad
+\mathrm{contender}_{20}:=F_{20}(F_{20}(42)),
+\]
+where \(L=\code{lTransformer}\).
+
+The full contender file has only six substantive definitions after the imports.
+There is no new object-language interpreter, type checker, or normalization
+argument.  All language-theoretic strength is inherited from Contender~19; all
+new work lies in the generic amplifier.
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{Formal comparison with Contender 19}
+
+We now unpack the chain~\eqref{eq:chain}.
+
+\subsection{The previous family is below raw reflection}
+
+Progressivity of the large-Veblen transformer states
+\[
+s(i,n)<L(s)(o,n)\qquad(i\le n).
+\]
+Taking \(s=s_{20}\), \(i=o=0\), and observing that
+\(s_{20}(0,n)=\mathrm{family19}(n)\), we obtain
+\[
+\mathrm{family19}(n)<L(s_{20})(0,n).
+\]
+This is \code{family19\_lt\_raw20}.
+
+\subsection{Raw reflection is below the finite seed lift}
+
+The existing theorem \code{diagonalLift\_strictlyInflationary} gives
+\[
+L(s_{20})(0,n)<D_{20}(n)(L)(s_{20})(0,n).
+\]
+This is \code{raw20\_lt\_seedLift20}.
+
+\subsection{The finite seed lift is below the W-rank lift}
+
+Corollary~\ref{cor:generic}, instantiated with schedule \(S_{20}(n)\), rank
+tree \(R_{20}(n)\), seed lift \(D_{20}(n)\), and base transformer \(L\), gives
+\[
+D_{20}(n)(L)(s_{20})(0,n)
+<W_{20}(n)(L)(s_{20})(n,n)=F_{20}(n).
+\]
+This is \code{seedLift20\_lt\_F20}.  Notice that this theorem isolates the new
+structural gain: even after the finite seed rank has been chosen using
+\(\mathrm{family19}(n)\), the outer W-tree stage is formally and strictly
+larger.
+
+\subsection{Pointwise and closed comparisons}
+
+By transitivity,
+\begin{theorem}[Pointwise dominance]
+For every \(n\),
+\[
+\mathrm{family19}(n)<F_{20}(n).
+\]
+\end{theorem}
+
+The existing input-growth theorem for \(F_{19}\) gives
+\[
+n<F_{19}(n)<F_{19}(F_{19}(n))=\mathrm{family19}(n)<F_{20}(n),
+\]
+so \(n<F_{20}(n)\) for every \(n\).  At \(n=42\),
+\[
+\mathrm{contender}_{19}=\mathrm{family19}(42)<F_{20}(42)
+<F_{20}(F_{20}(42))=\mathrm{contender}_{20}.
+\]
+Thus the proposed module states:
+
+\begin{lstlisting}
+theorem contender_19_lt_contender_20 :
+    contender_19 < contender_20 :=
+  Nat.lt_trans (family19_lt_F20 42)
+    (input_lt_F20 (F20 42))
+\end{lstlisting}
+
+The same file records the inherited corollary
+\(\mathrm{contender}_{18}<\mathrm{contender}_{20}\).
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{Bounding the large-Veblen programs}
+
+A contender should not merely dominate the previous closed numeral; it should
+retain a semantic explanation.  Contender~19 defines
+\code{FL.lveblenLadderUpTo n}, the finite enumeration of large-Veblen-ladder
+programs of depth at most \(n\), and \code{FL.lvalue} for their normalized
+natural-number values relative to a packed oracle family.
+
+The existing theorem says that every enumerated value is below one raw
+large-Veblen reflection:
+\[
+\code{FL.lvalue}(\code{packFamily}(s),n,t)
+<\code{reflectLFamily}(s,n).
+\]
+Combining it with raw reflection below \(F_{20}(n)\) yields:
+
+\begin{theorem}[Program bound]
+If \(t\in\code{FL.lveblenLadderUpTo n}\), then
+\[
+\code{FL.lvalue}(\code{packFamily}(s_{20}),n,t)<F_{20}(n).
+\]
+\end{theorem}
+
+This theorem, named \code{lvalue\_lt\_F20}, ties the new amplifier back to the
+strongest object language in the repository.  It also shows why reusing the
+large-Veblen reflector is preferable to an arbitrary arithmetic base: the
+number continues to dominate an explicit, certified class of programs.
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{Computability and termination}
+
+The number is computationally hopeless but constructive in the contest's
+sense.
+
+\subsection{Structural definitions}
+
+The new definitions recurse only on values whose inductive structure is
+visible:
+\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
+\item \code{runHyperLift} recurses on \code{WTree Nat};
+\item \code{Autonomous.hyper} recurses on a natural number and builds
+  \code{OTree} syntax;
+\item \code{Autonomous.realize} and \code{encode} recurse on tree syntax;
+\item \code{diagonalLift} recurses on a natural-number rank; and
+\item the inherited normalizer computes only from an explicit strong-
+  normalization certificate.
+\end{itemize}
+
+No new semantic termination axiom is introduced.  In particular, the outer
+rank tree is not traversed by a general while loop or a minimization operator;
+it is consumed by the recursor of an inductive W-type.
+
+\subsection{Countable branching is still data}
+
+A node of \code{WTree Nat} stores a total function \(\N\to\WTree(\N)\).  Such a
+function can describe infinitely many children, but it is itself a finite Lean
+term computing any requested child.  The inductive type rules out infinite
+descending branches.  This is exactly the representation already used by the
+repository's forest reflection and autonomous schedules.
+
+\subsection{Why evaluation should not be attempted}
+
+The fact that a term denotes a natural number and is computable in principle
+does not imply that reduction is remotely feasible.  Merely constructing the
+rank \(\mathrm{family19}(42)+1\) is beyond physical possibility, before any
+large-Veblen program normalization or outer W-rank traversal occurs.  The
+proper artifact is the definition and strict comparison theorem, not decimal
+or binary expansion.
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{How much larger is it?}
+
+There are two distinct answers.
+
+\subsection{The formal answer}
+
+The pointwise theorem
+\[
+\forall n\quad \mathrm{family19}(n)<F_{20}(n)
+\]
+is exact.  The proof also factors through two additional strict stages after
+raw reflection.  Therefore the new family is not merely larger at the final
+input; it uniformly dominates the self-composed Contender-19 family.
+
+Moreover, \code{seedLift20\_lt\_F20} proves that the new W-rank stage adds a
+strict amount even after the finite diagonal rank
+\(\mathrm{family19}(n)+1\) has been applied.  This isolates a formal advantage
+of the new mechanism over the strongest obvious same-type seed.
+
+\subsection{The heuristic answer}
+
+Let \(\Phi_S(L)=\Step_S(L)\).  Existing \code{diagonalLift S k} is finite
+iteration of a closely related successor operation beginning with autonomous
+lifting.  In contrast, \code{runHyperLift} supports a W-tree recursion:
+children are recursively evaluated, retained separately through a splice, and
+then passed through \(\Phi_S\).  A linear rank tree behaves like a long iterate
+of \(\Phi_S\); a limit-shaped node behaves like a join of a countable family of
+prior iterates followed by another step.
+
+This makes \code{treeDiagonalLift} a natural intensional analogue of
+transfinite iteration of \(\Phi_S\).  The rank tree \(R_{20}(n)\) is itself an
+autonomous hyperclosure above \(S_{20}(n)\), so the outer closure is far more
+than a fixed finite number of successor steps.
+
+\begin{caution}[No exact ordinal calibration is claimed]
+The operations manipulate program transformers that can inspect oracles.
+Their extensional behavior is nonmonotone, and the autonomous trees are syntax
+rather than quotient ordinals.  Assigning a precise fast-growing-hierarchy
+index would require a semantics of lift expressions and a simulation theorem.
+The report therefore uses ``W-rank'' and ``transfinite-shaped'' descriptively,
+not as a proved equality with a named ordinal.
+\end{caution}
+
+A conservative qualitative summary is:
+\begin{center}
+\begin{tabular}{lll}
+\toprule
+Construction & Rank object & Limit behavior\\
+\midrule
+Contender 15--19 amplifier & natural number & none at rank level\\
+Contender 20 seed & natural number \(\mathrm{family19}(n)+1\) & none at rank level\\
+Contender 20 outer stage & well-founded countably branching tree & splice of child lifts\\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{center}
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{Why the proposal is not a disguised finite rank}
+
+One might worry that every concrete Lean term is finite syntax, so the outer
+W-tree could secretly amount to one very large natural rank.  This objection
+confuses syntax size with inductive rank.  The term
+\[
+\mathsf{node}(i\mapsto\mathsf{chain}(i))
+\]
+is finite syntax but has children of every finite height.  There is no single
+finite branch-height bound.  More elaborate definable child functions produce
+far larger well-founded ranks.
+
+The outer recursion also retains child lifts separately.  Replacing a node by
+``iterate the same lift \(m\) times'' would discard this addressability.  In
+\code{runHyperLift}, later rank steps receive a splice and can select different
+child lifts by output coordinate.  Thus even a numerical upper bound on the
+height would not by itself establish extensional equivalence to
+\code{diagonalLift S k}.
+
+A fully formal separation theorem is left open.  Useful targets are:
+\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
+\item prove that chain-shaped rank trees simulate specified finite hyperlift
+  iterates;
+\item prove that a canonical omega-shaped rank tree dominates every fixed
+  finite iterate on a family of test transformers; and
+\item derive a non-representability result for W-rank lifts analogous to the
+  repository's earlier language non-representability results.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+The present contender does not need this separation theorem for strict
+comparison, because Theorem~\ref{thm:seedlt} already proves that the outer stage
+strictly exceeds its chosen finite seed lift.
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{Audit and engineering considerations}
+
+\subsection{Dependency discipline}
+
+The definition uses \code{F19} and \code{schedule19} but never the closed
+constant \code{contender\_19}.  This follows the repository's established
+pattern: each generation depends on the previous \emph{family}, not the
+previous final constant.  The focused audit traverses the environment closure
+of \code{contender\_20} and rejects dependencies on
+\code{contender\_5},\ldots,\code{contender\_19}.
+
+\subsection{Axiom audit}
+
+The package asks Lean to print axioms for:
+\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
+\item \code{contender\_20};
+\item \code{contender\_19\_lt\_contender\_20};
+\item \code{family19\_lt\_F20};
+\item \code{lvalue\_lt\_F20};
+\item \code{seed\_lt\_runHyperLift}; and
+\item \code{treeDiagonalLift\_good}.
+\end{itemize}
+The intended result is the repository's permitted set, at most
+\code{propext} and \code{Quot.sound}, with no \code{Classical.choice}.  The new
+proofs themselves use only induction, conjunction, definitional unfolding,
+and transitivity of \(<\).
+
+\subsection{Static audit already performed}
+
+The included \code{scripts/static\_audit.py} strips nested Lean comments and
+checks the three main modules for:
+\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
+\item balanced parentheses, brackets, and braces;
+\item forbidden shortcut tokens;
+\item SHA-256 digests and file sizes; and
+\item maximum line length.
+\end{itemize}
+The generated \code{STATIC\_AUDIT.json} reports \code{PASS}.  This is not a
+substitute for elaboration or kernel checking, but it catches packaging and
+obvious-source failures independently of Lean.
+
+\subsection{Expected build cost}
+
+The definition module adds no large inductive syntax, normalization semantics,
+or recursive proof over large-Veblen terms.  The generic module has fewer than
+200 lines, and the comparison module fewer than 90.  Most declarations are
+small structural recursors or theorem applications.  It is therefore plausible
+that the overlay fits the 15-second definition and 60-second proof budgets on
+the repository's reference class of machine.  This is a prediction, not a
+measurement; only a clean Lean~4.34.0 build can certify it.
+
+\subsection{Repository integration}
+
+The proposed integration steps are:
+\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
+\item copy the three \code{BusyLean/*.lean} files and the two \code{test/*.lean}
+  files into the repository;
+\item append the three imports supplied in
+  \code{integration/BusyLean.lean.append};
+\item in \code{scripts/check.py}, set
+  \begin{quote}\small
+  \code{PREVIOUS = "BusyLean.Comparison19"}\\
+  \code{CURRENT\_DEF = "BusyLean.Contender20"}.
+  \end{quote}
+\item build \code{BusyLean.Contender20} and
+  \code{BusyLean.Comparison20};
+\item run the smoke and focused audit files; and
+\item after success, merge the focused audit declarations into the repository's
+  global \code{test/Audit.lean}.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{Verification boundary and risk register}
+
+A serious proposal should separate proved mathematics, source-level evidence,
+and unverified assumptions.
+
+\begin{longtable}{P{0.25\textwidth}P{0.27\textwidth}P{0.39\textwidth}}
+\toprule
+Claim & Current evidence & Remaining action\\
+\midrule
+\endfirsthead
+\toprule
+Claim & Current evidence & Remaining action\\
+\midrule
+\endhead
+Definitions are constructive & All new recursors are structural; static audit
+finds no shortcut tokens & Confirm generated declaration kinds and axioms in Lean\\
+The generic strictness proof is logically sound & Full proof given in
+Sections 5--8 and encoded in Lean & Elaborate and kernel-check on 4.34.0\\
+The imports and names match the base repository & Source was written against
+commit \code{64d31a0...} and exact current interfaces & Build overlay against
+that commit\\
+The proof stays within axiom policy & No new classical argument; audit commands
+included & Inspect \code{\#print axioms} output\\
+The closure avoids earlier contender constants & Definition uses \code{F19},
+not \code{contender\_19}; closure command included & Run environment closure audit\\
+The time budgets are met & Modules are small and reuse compiled theory & Run
+clean budget script on reference hardware\\
+The outer rank is qualitatively beyond finite rank & W-tree recursion and
+spliced limit nodes are explicit & Formalize finite-iterate simulation and
+separation theorems\\
+A precise ordinal calibration is known & Not claimed & Develop semantics for
+hyperlift expressions\\
+\bottomrule
+\end{longtable}
+
+The largest practical risk is not the mathematics but elaboration detail:
+definitional reduction around \code{spliceLifts} or a changed implicit argument
+could require a local \code{change} or \code{simpa}.  The proof was written in
+the style of the repository's current \code{HyperReflectionFacts.lean}, and
+all such equalities are shallow.  No repair should alter the mathematical
+definitions.
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{Further research program}
+
+The proposal opens several directions that are more interesting than merely
+repeating it with a larger seed.
+
+\subsection{Immediate formal tasks}
+
+\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
+\item \textbf{Kernel build and timing.}  Produce a clean Lean~4.34.0 receipt,
+  axiom report, closure count, and definition/proof timings.
+\item \textbf{Chain simulation.}  Define a finite chain tree \(C_k\)
+  and prove an explicit recurrence for the stage
+  \[
+  \Run(\Step_S,L,C_k).
+  \]
+\item \textbf{Canonical limit tree.}  Define
+  \(C_\omega=\mathsf{node}(k\mapsto C_k)\) and prove that its child coordinates
+  expose every finite stage.
+\item \textbf{Rank monotonicity without oracle monotonicity.}  Identify an
+  intensional embedding relation on lift expressions under which replacing a
+  rank tree by a tree containing it preserves a strict lower bound.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\subsection{Semantics and ordinal analysis}
+
+Assign formal syntax to transformer, lift, and hyperlift expressions, with an
+interpretation into the existing functions.  A well-founded rank semantics
+could then prove lower bounds of the form
+\[
+\text{expression rank }\alpha\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
+\text{dominates a calibrated hierarchy through }\alpha.
+\]
+The nonmonotone oracle semantics means that the usual pointwise order is too
+coarse.  A logical relation tracking explicit seed occurrences and selectable
+child coordinates is a more promising invariant.
+
+\subsection{Higher type levels}
+
+Contender~20 adds one fixed type level, \(\HLift=\Lift\to\Lift\).  A systematic
+successor would code a hierarchy
+\[
+X_0=\Tr,\qquad X_{k+1}=X_k\to X_k
+\]
+and interpret generic splice/stage syntax at each finite level.  The level
+could then depend on the input.  This would parallel the earlier transition
+from fixed F$_\omega$ kinds to dynamic and laddered universes, but on the
+amplifier axis rather than the object-language axis.
+
+\subsection{Reflecting the amplifier language}
+
+A stronger and more difficult construction would give the object language
+codes for good transformer lifts or hyperlifts, normalize those codes, and
+reflect over every bounded program defining such an amplifier.  This would
+replace the hand-selected \code{rankStep} by diagonalization over an explicit
+amplifier language.  Soundness would require a typed syntax whose semantics
+preserves \(\Good\), making the invariant itself part of the type system.
+
+\subsection{Collapsing-function universe ladders}
+
+The language axis still has room above large Veblen.  A canonical system of
+Buchholz-style or operator-controlled collapsing terms, with Lean-checked
+normal forms and well-foundedness, could feed \code{FL.wfUniv}.  The main
+challenge is eliminating descending chains admitted by raw terms.  Once such a
+ladder exists, it composes immediately with the W-rank amplifier developed
+here.
+
+\subsection{Minimality and compression}
+
+The generic module is intentionally explicit.  After a successful build, one
+can investigate whether \code{GoodLift} should be a structure, whether the two
+strictness predicates admit a common typeclass, and whether the first- and
+second-order stage proofs can be generated from a single universe-polymorphic
+schema.  Compression should follow validation, not precede it.
+
+%======================================================================
+\section{Conclusion}
+
+Contender~20 is designed as a principled next step rather than a cosmetic
+increase.  Contender~19's language remains the base: programs range over a
+large-Veblen-length universe ladder with certified normalization.  Its
+natural-number rank hierarchy remains as an enormous seed.  The new operation
+raises the amplifier one type level and closes it under structural recursion on
+countably branching well-founded trees.
+
+The core theorem is robust because it uses only explicit occurrences.  At an
+outer node, child zero is below the parent by induction and second-order strict
+inflation; the splice makes that child definitionally visible.  No oracle
+monotonicity, ordinal quotient, or semantic shortcut enters the formal
+comparison.  This yields the exact pointwise inequality
+\(\mathrm{family19}(n)<F_{20}(n)\), the closed comparison
+\(\mathrm{contender}_{19}<\mathrm{contender}_{20}\), and a retained upper bound
+for every enumerated large-Veblen-ladder program.
+
+The proposal's mathematical architecture is complete and the source package is
+ready for integration.  Its one unresolved status item is mechanical but
+essential: the supplied Lean code must be built and audited on the repository's
+Lean~4.34.0 toolchain.  The package makes that boundary visible rather than
+substituting confidence for a kernel receipt.
+
